Mike Kelly wrote:

> In what concievable way does a high score indicate good play?

In my personal games, on a score file only containing my game
scores, higher scores correlate to greater progress through the
game. It's possible to "game for score", but I don't, against
myself. I'm way miffed when a game ends and I've not been able
to beat even the lowest score on the record file, because that
means I'm playing _worse_ than the worst of my best hundred
games. Sadly, that is the case about 7/8ths of the time, so
every once in a while I get disgusted, and empty out the record
file while keeping the logfile, just so that some of my games
still seem competitive.

So, for me, higher score is still a very important metric of game
quality, and that's probably true for most players who've never
ascended.

